// Packed fp4 routes through the cube, and the operand contract the two matmul
// ops are held to.
//
// tla.mmad and tla.mmad_mx are both written by the user and neither is inferred
// from the other, so each has to reject the operands belonging to the other.
// That check lives in the frontend, which is why these two cases fail while the
// IR is still being generated rather than while it is being compiled.

// RUN: not %python %S/Inputs/generate_mmad_ir.py mx-half-scaled 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=HALFSCALED

// HALFSCALED: lhs operand was loaded by tla.copy(..., scale=...)

// RUN: not %python %S/Inputs/generate_mmad_ir.py mx-op-without-scale 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=MXNOSCALE

// MXNOSCALE: lhs operand was not loaded by tla.copy(..., scale=...)

// The encoding of a packed fp4 tile is its element type, so the load and the
// matmul cannot disagree -- there is no second place for it to be stated. The
// two operands need not agree with each other either: the cube has a mad_mx for
// every pairing, so a mixed-encoding matmul is legal and names both types in its
// callee.

// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/generate_mmad_ir.py mxfp4-mixed-encodings | %tla_compile - -o - 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=FMTMIXED

// FMTMIXED: mmad_float4_e1m2x2_t_float4_e2m1x2_t_float

// Packed fp4 is microscaling-only: there is no plain fp4 route on the cube. An
// unscaled fp4 load leaves tiles that are i8 as far as MLIR is concerned, so
// without this the matmul would fall through to the generic "unsupported element
// types" complaint and name i8 rather than the actual problem.

// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/generate_mmad_ir.py mxfp4-unscaled | not %tla_compile - -o - 2>&1 | %filecheck %s --check-prefix=FP4UNSCALED

// FP4UNSCALED: packed fp4 tla.mmad operands must be loaded by tla.copy(..., scale=...); the cube has no non-microscaling fp4 route
